BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucross-fellowship-native-american-visual-artists-spring-2027@artificial
 nouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ucross Fellowship for Native American Visual Artists: Spr
 ing 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Ucross Fellowship for Native American Visual Artists is a d
 edicated fellowship within Ucross Foundation's residency program\, offerin
 g fully funded time and space to make work on a working cattle ranch in th
 e foothills of Wyoming's Bighorn Mountains. The fellowship provides living
  accommodations\, meals\, and work space at no charge\, with no applicatio
 n fee. One Fellowship winner is selected per session\; all applicants also
  have the option of being considered for a general Ucross residency. ELIGI
 BILITY: open to Native American visual artists who are practicing contempo
 rary artists currently producing work in any visual medium\, including but
  not limited to painting\, drawing\, mixed media\, sculpture\, printmaking
 \, photography\, video\, animation\, performance art\, installation\, and 
 ceramics\; and who are enrolled members of a state-recognized or federally
 -recognized Tribe\, Pueblo\, Nation\, Native Community\, Political Entity\
 , or Alaskan Native Village. The work sample and project description are t
 he most significant features of the application. Unless the work is interd
 isciplinary\, applicants are encouraged to apply in a single primary disci
 pline and submit a work sample and project description emphasizing that di
 scipline. Work-sample requirements: VISUAL ARTWORK\, submit 10 images (JPE
 G format\, no PDFs) with file names including title and size\, plus a data
  sheet listing title\, medium\, size\, and year of completion for each ima
 ge\; FILM/VIDEO/ANIMATION/PERFORMANCE\, submit up to 3 video files (each u
 nder 8 minutes and smaller than 200 MB) or up to 3 Vimeo or YouTube links.
  The open call for the Spring 2027 session closes July 15\, 2026 (apply by
  11:59 p.m. MT). A separate Ucross Fellowship for Native American Writers 
 is also offered.\n\nOrganisation: Ucross Foundation\nLocation: Sheridan\, 
 Wyoming\, USA\nAward: Fully funded fellowship residency\; living accommoda
 tions\, meals\, and work space provided. No application fee.\nDuration: Sp
 ring session (February through early June 2027)\; residencies range from t
 wo to six weeks\n\nMore: https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-progra
 m.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-hodder-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Hodder Fellowship 2027-2028 (Princeton University)
DESCRIPTION:The Hodder Fellowship is given to artists and writers of except
 ional promise to pursue independent projects at Princeton University durin
 g the academic year. Potential Hodder Fellows are composers\, choreographe
 rs\, performance artists\, visual artists\, writers\, translators\, or oth
 er kinds of artists or humanists who have 'much more than ordinary intelle
 ctual and literary gifts'\; they are selected more 'for promise than for p
 erformance.' Given the strength of the applicant pool\, most successful Fe
 llows have published a first book or have similar achievements in their ow
 n fields\; the Hodder is designed to provide Fellows with the 'studious le
 isure' to undertake significant new work. Fellows spend an academic year a
 t Princeton with no formal teaching\, supported by a USD 93\,000 stipend f
 or the 10-month Visiting Fellow appointment. IMPORTANT: for this cycle\, a
 pplications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Program in Th
 eater and Music Theater\, the Program in Visual Arts\, and the Department 
 of Music. Fellowships are not intended to fund work leading to an advanced
  degree\; one need not be a U.S. citizen to apply\; previous recipients of
  the Princeton Arts Fellowship are not eligible. To apply\, submit a resum
 e\, a 500-word project proposal\, and samples of your work (writing sample
 \, images\, video links to performances\, etc.). Applicants may apply for 
 the Hodder Fellowship only twice in a lifetime. Applications are submitted
  via Interfolio. The application cycle closes 8 September 2026 at 11:59 p.
 m. EDT.\n\nOrganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\
 nLocation: Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA\; academic-year appointment as a V
 isiting Fellow (no U.S. citizenship required)\nAward: USD 93\,000 stipend 
 for the 10-month appointment as a Visiting Fellow. No formal teaching is i
 nvolved. No application fee.\nDuration: One academic year (10-month appoin
 tment)\, 2027-2028\n\nMore: https://arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/hodder-
 fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/hodder-fellowship/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA\; academic-year appointment as a Visi
 ting Fellow (no U.S. citizenship required)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:accent-sisters-work-study-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260801
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:Deadline: Accent Sisters Work-Study Fellowship Program 2026
DESCRIPTION:Accent Sisters offers a work-study fellowship for individuals p
 assionate about literature\, visual arts\, and community engagement. The f
 ellowship provides hands-on experience in a bookstore and a hybrid event s
 pace where exhibitions\, book launches\, film screenings\, educational pro
 grams\, and other public programming take place. It is ideal for someone l
 ooking to build a career in cultural institutions\, independent bookstores
 \, or art galleries. Fellows work closely with both the bookstore and gall
 ery teams\, gaining skills in book curation\, exhibit planning\, marketing
 \, and community outreach\, while developing a personal project that merge
 s these disciplines. WHAT YOU GET: opportunities to work with writers\, ar
 tists\, and creators\; a chance to pitch projects\, events\, and programs 
 for a diverse community\; free passes to all in-person events and classes\
 ; fellow discounts\; a showcase of your personal project toward the end of
  the fellowship\; one-on-one mentorship with the Accent team\; and a USD 6
 00 stipend. Open to upcoming seniors\, upcoming second-year graduate stude
 nts\, and recent graduates of undergraduate/graduate programs. Four tracks
  are offered and applicants generally choose one: Program Coordination & C
 ommunity Engagement\; Curation & Administration\; Graphic Design & Digital
  Asset Management\; or Marketing & Business Development. The fellowship ru
 ns 20 August to 20 December 2026 (part-time\, 3 days per week\, on-site re
 quired). Applications are reviewed from 15 July to 1 August 2026.\n\nOrgan
 isation: Accent Sisters\nLocation: 89 5th Ave\, Suite 702\, New York\, NY 
 10003 (hybrid\; part-time\, 3 days/week\, on-site work required)\nAward: S
 tipend of USD 600 (paid in two installments)\, plus free passes to all in-
 person events and classes\, fellow discounts\, and one-on-one mentorship.\
 nDuration: 20 August to 20 December 2026\; part-time\, 3 days per week\n\n
 More: https://www.accentaccent.com/page/fellowship?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.accentaccent.com/page/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:89 5th Ave\, Suite 702\, New York\, NY 10003 (hybrid\; part-time\,
  3 days/week\, on-site work required)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:target-margin-tmt-institute-fellowship-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260722
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260723
SUMMARY:Deadline: Target Margin Theater: TMT Institute Fellowship 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:Target Margin Theater invites applications for its next cohort 
 of TMT Institute Fellows for the 2026/2027 season. The Institute is a coho
 rt of artists who seek to radically disrupt and re-examine their existing 
 practice without any goal or expected result: pure experimentation with no
  product-oriented end goal. Fellows pursue no project\, no developmental s
 tage\, no deliverable\; misfires\, dead ends\, and bad ideas are considere
 d a fruitful part of the work. SUPPORT: TMT provides space\, material reso
 urces\, collaborators\, and a USD 2\,000 stipend. STRUCTURE: three short i
 ntensive sessions at Target Margin Theater in Brooklyn (5-8 November 2026\
 , 8-10 January 2027\, and 19-21 March 2027\, all day with some evening hou
 rs)\, monthly breakfasts at Junior's in Brooklyn (20 October\, 30 November
 \, 4 January\, 1 February\, 8 March\, 5 April\, 3 May)\, and occasional ex
 cursions. After the second intensive\, each Fellow commits to an explorati
 on pursued outside group gatherings\, supported by TMT resources. The seas
 on ends with an Open Studio weekend in June 2027 to share explorations wit
 h the community\; this is not a performance and should not be an audience-
 attended\, time-bounded show. WHO CAN APPLY: artists from any discipline w
 ithin or outside theater\, as well as production\, technical\, and adminis
 trative workers in the arts. Applicants must have an established\, existin
 g body of work and practice they are committed to changing\; do not apply 
 with a project. Recent graduates of training programs are unlikely to be a
 ppropriate candidates\; no formal education level or degree is required. S
 ELECTION: TMT staff and artistic community review applications and invite 
 a handful of candidates to interview\; all applicants are notified by Octo
 ber. Deadline: Wednesday 22 July 2026\, 11:59 pm EST.\n\nOrganisation: Tar
 get Margin Theater\nLocation: Brooklyn\, New York\, United States (Sunset 
 Park). Fellows must be available in person for intensives\, monthly breakf
 asts\, and excursions.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend\, plus space\, material 
 resources\, and collaborators to support each Fellow's experiments. No app
 lication fee.\nDuration: 2026-27 season: three intensive sessions in Brook
 lyn (5-8 November 2026\, 8-10 January 2027\, 19-21 March 2027)\, monthly b
 reakfasts October 2026 through May 2027\, and an Open Studio weekend in Ju
 ne 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.targetmargin.org/institute/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.targetmargin.org/institute/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brooklyn\, New York\, United States (Sunset Park). Fellows must be
  available in person for intensives\, monthly breakfasts\, and excursions.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adrianna-newell-fellowship-artsrec-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Adrianna Newell Fellowship at Arts&Rec 2026
DESCRIPTION:Inaugural Adrianna Newell Fellowship\, a live/work studio resid
 ency in Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills) for October 2026\, with possible
  extension into November. Named for board member Adrianna Newell\, it invi
 tes queer lens-based artists working in photography and moving image with 
 an active\, research-driven or conceptually rich practice\, especially wor
 k engaging queer perspectives on land\, rurality and regional space\, or q
 ueer identity\, memory\, intimacy\, community and embodiment. Open to arti
 sts from everywhere. Fellows receive a 1000 sq ft studio with attached apa
 rtment\, a $1\,500 combined materials stipend and per diem\, and introduct
 ions to the local creative community\; an open-studio presentation\, artis
 t talk or public exhibition at the end is required. Travel\, especially in
 ternational\, is not funded (residents are encouraged to seek travel suppo
 rt elsewhere). Apply by emailing a single PDF (one-page description of you
 r work and planned project\, 10 to 20 work samples with short descriptions
 \, and a current CV) to programs@artsrec.org. Deadline: 31 July 2026\; not
 ices by end of August.\n\nOrganisation: Arts&Rec Inc\nLocation: Stamford\,
  NY (Western Catskills)\, USA\nAward: Live/work residency: use of a 1000 s
 q ft studio and attached apartment\, plus a $1\,500 total materials stipen
 d and per diem. Travel (especially international) is not funded. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: October 2026 (four weeks to two months\, based on pr
 oject needs\; possible extension into November)\n\nMore: https://www.nyfa.
 org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-applications%3A-the-adr
 ianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&id=013e03ad-6edf-47f
 b-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-app
 lications%3A-the-adrianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&
 id=013e03ad-6edf-47fb-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills)\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bavc-mediamaker-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship is a ten-month intensive for doc
 umentary filmmakers using bold cinematic language and innovative impact st
 rategies to take on critical issues of the moment. Each fellow receives US
 D 10\,000 in unrestricted funding paid out across the programme\, plus tra
 vel per diems\, and joins a cohort programme of workshops\, feedback sessi
 ons\, mentorship and industry access rather than a one-off cash award. ELI
 GIBILITY: directors of a first or second feature-length documentary\, base
 d primarily in the United States. The project must be non-commercial and f
 eature-length (shorts\, series and podcasts are excluded)\, primarily docu
 mentary or non-fiction including hybrid forms\, and demonstrate potential 
 for social impact. Only one applicant per filmmaking team may apply\, and 
 full-time students are not eligible. PROJECT STAGE: eligible films must ha
 ve at least begun production and may have started post-production\, but sh
 ould not reach a fine cut before the programme year ends\, since the point
  is to workshop the film in progress. APPLICATION: the first step is an In
 tent to Apply\, submitted through the Airtable form linked from the progra
 mme page\, due 30 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: BAVC Media (Bay Ar
 ea Video Coalition)\nLocation: United States\; applicants must primarily r
 eside in the U.S. and not expect to live abroad for a major part of the ye
 ar. Mostly remote\, with two in-person convenings in San Francisco (travel
  covered).\nAward: USD 10\,000 distributed to each Fellow over the course 
 of the fellowship\, plus travel per diems. Non-cash support includes fligh
 ts and accommodation for two San Francisco convenings\, festival and confe
 rence access with travel covered\, professional mentorship and strategy se
 ssions\, five hours of one-to-one mentorship through MediaMaker Connect\, 
 and ongoing workshops. Bay Area fellows also get access to BAVC's co-worki
 ng space and facilities. No application fee.\nDuration: Ten-month fellowsh
 ip\n\nMore: https://bavc.org/programs/mediamaker/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://bavc.org/programs/mediamaker/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; applicants must primarily reside in the U.S. and n
 ot expect to live abroad for a major part of the year. Mostly remote\, wit
 h two in-person convenings in San Francisco (travel covered).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:laselle-travel-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Travel Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation established this fell
 owship in recognition of the impact travel can have on an artist's career.
  Two awards of USD 5\,000 go to artists working and residing in Texas\, en
 abling each recipient to complete a research trip that furthers their arti
 stic practice. The fellowship echoes its namesake's own history: Dorothy A
 ntoinette 'Toni' LaSelle\, a pioneer of Modernism in Texas\, travelled to 
 Europe in 1927-28 after completing her MA at the University of Chicago\, e
 xploring the art of England\, Italy and France on a trip made possible by 
 a study fund awarded for work in the First Student Art Exhibition at the R
 enaissance Society in 1926. JUROR: this year's guest juror is Lana Meador\
 , Associate Curator of Modern and Contemporary Art at the San Antonio Muse
 um of Art\, who joined SAMA's curatorial department in 2015 and has organi
 sed more than a dozen exhibitions there\, including co-curating Texas Wome
 n: A New History of Abstract Art (2020) and the ongoing Gateway series of 
 commissioned site-specific murals. She has juried artist awards for the Ci
 ty of San Antonio's Department of Arts and Culture\, the International Col
 or Awards\, Texas Sculpture Group and the Utah Artist Fellowship. ELIGIBIL
 ITY: must currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year
 \; must be a practising professional with at least three solo or group exh
 ibitions in the past three years (BFA and juried exhibitions count)\; and 
 must currently hold a degree in the arts. APPLICATION MATERIALS: image thu
 mbnails of a maximum of four works with title\, date\, medium and dimensio
 ns (JPEG or JPG only\, at least 1\,200 pixels on the longest side and unde
 r 5MB each)\; an artist CV\; a reference contact list with two names\, con
 tact details and relationship to the artist\; a project proposal of 2\,000
  characters or less covering why the fellowship matters to your practice\,
  your goals for the travel\, and when the proposed travel would take place
 \; and a copy of a valid Texas driver's licence or other proof of one year
  of Texas residency. All information and communications are in English\, w
 ith translation assistance available on request. Applications opened 3 Aug
 ust 2026\; awardees are notified 2 November 2026. Questions: info@lasellef
 oundation.org. Deadline 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm CDT.\n\nOrganisation: Do
 rothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation\nLocation: Texas\, USA. Applicants mus
 t currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year. The fu
 nded research trip itself may be anywhere.\nAward: Two awards of USD 5\,00
 0 each\, distributed in the fall of 2026\, to fund a research trip that fu
 rthers the artist's practice. No application fee.\nDuration: Travel must b
 e completed between 1 December 2026 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://
 www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Texas\, USA. Applicants must currently reside in Texas and have li
 ved there for the past year. The funded research trip itself may be anywhe
 re.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asf-fellowships-americans-nordic-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T175145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: American-Scandinavian Foundation: Fellowships for America
 ns in the Nordic Countries 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:The American-Scandinavian Foundation's award program for study 
 and research abroad is its longest-standing commitment to educational exch
 ange: over the past 100 years\, more than 4\,000 fellowships and grants ha
 ve gone to Americans working in the Nordic region and Scandinavians in the
  US engaged in study\, research\, or creative arts projects. Fellowships f
 or Americans in the Nordic Countries support projects in Denmark\, the Far
 oe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Finland\, Norway\, Sweden\, and Sapmi. 
 AMOUNTS: long-term fellowships of up to USD 23\,000 for 4 to 12 months\, a
 nd short-term grants of up to USD 5\,000 for 1 to 3 months\; awards are gi
 ven in all fields\, with graduate students (preferably dissertation-relate
 d)\, academic professionals\, and creative artists eligible. In 2026\, 31 
 American Fellows were granted over USD 277\,390 in total. REPORTING: fello
 ws abroad more than six months submit an interim report mid-project\, and 
 all fellows submit a final narrative report within 60 days of the fellowsh
 ip period's end. DEADLINE: 1 November 2026 for the 2027-28 cycle. See ASF'
 s Program Guidelines for full requirements and apply via the ASF applicati
 on portal.\n\nOrganisation: American-Scandinavian Foundation\nLocation: No
 rdic region (Denmark\, Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Finland\, Nor
 way\, Sweden\, and Sapmi). For American graduate students\, academic profe
 ssionals\, and creative artists\; project must take place in the Nordic co
 untries.\nAward: Long-term fellowships of up to USD 23\,000 for 4 to 12 mo
 nths\; short-term grants of up to USD 5\,000 for 1 to 3 months. In 2026\, 
 31 American Fellows received over USD 277\,390 in total funding. Awards ar
 e given in all fields\, including creative arts projects.\nDuration: 1 to 
 3 months (grants) or 4 to 12 months (fellowships)\; year-long fellows subm
 it an interim report\, and all fellows submit a final narrative report wit
 hin 60 days of the fellowship's end.\n\nMore: https://www.amscan.org/fello
 wships-grants/grants-and-awards-for-americans/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.amscan.org/fellowships-grants/grants-and-awards-for-america
 ns/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nordic region (Denmark\, Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Fin
 land\, Norway\, Sweden\, and Sapmi). For American graduate students\, acad
 emic professionals\, and creative artists\; project must take place in the
  Nordic countries.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
